{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 10, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Here is a 300+ word summary of the Wikipedia page \"Raymond III Count of Tripoli\" without commas and with at least 3 highlighted sections in markdown format:\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Saint-Germain and ruled as Count of Tripoli from 1147 to 1183. He was the son of William II Count of Tripoli and Beatrice of Jerusalem. Raymond III succeeded his father at a young age and was initially under the regency of his mother.\n\n*Raymond III played a key role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was a strong opponent of King Amalric I. He refused to provide military support to the king during the invasion of Fatimid Egypt in 1167 and instead focused on securing his own county.*\n\nRaymond III was taken prisoner by Nur ad-Din during the Battle of Inab in 1154. He was released in 1156 after his mother paid a large ransom. Upon his release Raymond III worked to strengthen the defenses of Tripoli and formed alliances with the Crusader states.\n\n*The County of Tripoli under Raymond III experienced a period of relative peace and prosperity. He encouraged trade and commerce and the county became an important center for the production of silk and other textiles.*\n\nIn 1177 Raymond III was appointed as Regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem following the death of King Amalric I. He played a crucial role in the Battle of Montgisard where the Crusaders defeated the Ayyubid forces led by Saladin.\n\n*Raymond III was known for his military prowess and his ability to navigate the complex politics of the Crusader states. He died in 1183 and was succeeded by his cousin Bohemond III of Antioch.* \n\nRaymond III death marked the end of the Saint-Germain line in Tripoli. His legacy continued to influence the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem for many years after his death. The county of Tripoli remained an important Crusa